# Video 41 · angle 2 (a stage of life, the grandparents of the baby boom) · afternoon slot · day 14
# What Being a Grandparent in 1960 Was Really Like

sc("""If you grew up in the fifties or sixties, close your eyes and picture your grandmother's house. The smell of something baking. A candy dish on the table. A clock ticking in the hallway. Crocheted doilies on every chair. And your grandmother, in a cotton housedress and an apron, waiting at the door to hug you. For millions of baby boomers, that house was the safest place in the world.""",
   "1950s grandmother kitchen baking", "GRANDMA'S HOUSE")
sc("""But what was life like for the grandparents themselves? If you were a grandparent in nineteen sixty, you were probably born in the eighteen nineties or the first years of the nineteen hundreds. You had grown up with horses and buggies, oil lamps and outhouses. And now you were watching your grandchildren grow up with television, jet airplanes and rockets to space.""",
   "1950s elderly couple portrait", "BORN IN THE 1890s")
sc("""Today, let's look at the world through their eyes. The grandparents of the baby boom. What they had lived through, how they lived in nineteen sixty, and what they gave their grandchildren. If you had grandparents like these, I think you'll see them clearly by the end.""",
   "1950s grandparents grandchildren porch", "THROUGH THEIR EYES")

ch("What they had lived through")
sc("""Think about what a grandparent of nineteen sixty had already lived through. As children, many had no electricity, no telephone and no car. They were teenagers or young adults during the First World War. Many young men served in France in nineteen seventeen and eighteen. And then came the influenza pandemic of nineteen eighteen, which killed about six hundred and seventy-five thousand Americans.""",
   "1910s family portrait farm", "THEY HAD LIVED THROUGH IT ALL")
sc("""Then came the Great Depression. Many of them were in their thirties when the stock market crashed in nineteen twenty-nine. They lost jobs, farms, savings and homes. They raised their children on beans, bread and hand-me-downs. Many never fully trusted banks again, and kept cash hidden in coffee cans, mattresses and cookie jars for the rest of their lives.""",
   "1930s Depression family", "THE GREAT DEPRESSION")
sc("""And then the Second World War, when they sent their own sons off to fight, and hung a blue star in the window. Some of those stars turned gold. By nineteen sixty, they had survived two world wars, a pandemic and a depression. No wonder they seemed so calm about the small problems of their grandchildren.""",
   "1940s mother service flag window", "A STAR IN THE WINDOW")
sc("""They had also seen the world change faster than any generation before. They had seen the first automobiles, the first airplanes, the first radios and the first movies with sound. They had watched electric lights come to their towns, and indoor plumbing come to their homes. Some of them had seen their first television set only a few years earlier, in their sixties.""",
   "1910s early automobile street horses", "THEY SAW IT ALL CHANGE")

ch("Where they lived")
sc("""In nineteen sixty, many grandparents still lived in the old family home. A big farmhouse, or a two-story house in an older part of town, with a front porch, a big kitchen and a garden out back. They had lived there for thirty or forty years. Every room was full of memories, and nothing ever changed.""",
   "1950s old two story house front porch", "THE OLD FAMILY HOME")
sc("""Many others lived with their grown children. It was common for a widowed grandmother to move in with her son or daughter's family. She might have a small bedroom upstairs, or a room off the kitchen. She helped cook, watched the children and told stories. Three generations under one roof was a normal part of American life.""",
   "1950s grandmother living with family", "THREE GENERATIONS")
sc("""Some grandparents moved to Florida or Arizona, but in nineteen sixty, that was still unusual. Most stayed close to home, close to their church and their friends, and close to their grandchildren. On Sundays, the whole family came over for dinner. The grandparents' house was the center of the family.""",
   "1950s family Sunday dinner grandparents", "THE CENTER OF THE FAMILY")

ch("Money")
sc("""Money was tight for many grandparents in nineteen sixty. Social Security had only started paying monthly benefits in nineteen forty, and in nineteen sixty, the average check for a retired worker was about seventy-four dollars a month. Many older people had no pension at all. And there was no Medicare yet. That wouldn't come until nineteen sixty-five.""",
   "1950s elderly woman mailbox check", "$74 A MONTH")
sc("""In nineteen fifty-nine, about one in three Americans over sixty-five lived in poverty. Many depended on their children for help. Others kept working as long as their bodies allowed, as farmers, shopkeepers, carpenters or seamstresses. The idea of retiring at sixty-five and traveling the world was still new.""",
   "1950s elderly man working shop", "ONE IN THREE WAS POOR")
sc("""But they were masters at making do. Grandmothers saved string, rubber bands, bread bags, aluminum foil and buttons. They mended socks, patched clothes, and made quilts from scraps. They grew vegetables and canned them. Nothing was thrown away that might be useful someday. Use it up, wear it out, make it do, or do without.""",
   "1950s grandmother quilting", "USE IT UP, WEAR IT OUT")
sc("""Many grandparents also had firm opinions about money. Never buy what you can't pay cash for. Always keep something put away for a rainy day. Don't trust anything that sounds too good to be true. They watched their grown children buy cars and televisions on credit, and shook their heads. Their grandchildren didn't always listen, but many later wished they had.""",
   "1950s elderly man counting money", "PAY CASH")

ch("A day with grandma")
sc("""What was a day like for a grandmother in nineteen sixty? She rose early, out of a lifetime's habit. She cooked a big breakfast, with eggs, bacon, biscuits and coffee boiled in an old pot. She swept the porch, fed the chickens if she had them, and worked in her garden while it was still cool.""",
   "1950s elderly woman garden morning", "UP EARLY")
sc("""In the afternoon, she might bake. Bread, pies, cookies, cakes. Many grandmothers never measured anything. A handful of this, a pinch of that, until it looked right. Their grandchildren later tried to write down the recipes, and found it almost impossible. The secret ingredient, many said, was the grandmother herself.""",
   "1950s grandmother baking pie kitchen", "SHE NEVER MEASURED")
sc("""In the evening, she listened to the radio or watched television. Lawrence Welk on Saturday nights was a favorite of many grandparents. So were Ed Sullivan, Gunsmoke and religious programs. She read the Bible, did crossword puzzles, and wrote letters to her children and grandchildren in careful, flowing cursive.""",
   "1950s elderly couple watching television", "LAWRENCE WELK")
sc("""And she went to church. For many grandmothers, the church was the center of life outside the home. Sunday services, Wednesday prayer meetings, the ladies' aid society, the quilting circle and the church suppers. She brought casseroles to every funeral and pies to every bake sale. Her faith had carried her through the hardest times, and she never let go of it.""",
   "1950s elderly women church", "HER FAITH")

ch("A day with grandpa")
sc("""Grandfathers of nineteen sixty were often quiet men with strong hands. Many had worked on farms, in mines, on railroads or in factories since they were boys. They had pocket watches and suspenders, and a pocketknife they used for everything. Many chewed tobacco or smoked a pipe.""",
   "1950s elderly man pipe portrait", "STRONG HANDS")
sc("""Grandpa might spend his mornings in the workshop, fixing things for the neighbors. His afternoons might be at the barbershop, the feed store or the courthouse square, where the old men gathered on benches to talk about the weather, the crops and the old days. In the evening, he sat on the porch in his rocking chair.""",
   "1950s old men sitting bench town square", "THE BENCH ON THE SQUARE")
sc("""He took the grandchildren fishing. He taught them to whittle, to bait a hook, to drive a nail and to whistle. He told stories about the war, or the Depression, or what the town looked like before the paved roads came. And he slipped them a quarter or a stick of gum when their parents weren't looking.""",
   "1950s grandfather grandson fishing", "GONE FISHING")
sc("""Grandparents also had very definite ideas about manners. Children said yes ma'am and no sir. You didn't speak at the table unless spoken to. You wrote a thank-you note for every gift, in your best handwriting. You stood up when an adult entered the room. And if you forgot, you heard about it, gently but firmly.""",
   "1950s children grandparents living room", "YES MA'AM")

ch("The grandchildren")
sc("""For the grandchildren, visits to grandma's house were magical. The cookie jar was always full. The rules were looser. Grandma let you stay up late, eat dessert first, and lick the bowl. You slept in a big feather bed under a handmade quilt. In the morning, you woke up to the smell of bacon and fresh coffee.""",
   "1950s child grandmother cookie jar", "THE COOKIE JAR")
sc("""The house was full of interesting things. An old trunk in the attic with army uniforms and love letters. A pump organ in the parlor. A wind-up Victrola with old records. Photographs of stern-looking ancestors on the walls. A button tin, full of every button ever saved, that children could sort for hours.""",
   "old attic trunk antique items", "THE BUTTON TIN")
sc("""Many grandparents also spoke another language. German, Italian, Polish, Swedish, Yiddish, Czech or Spanish. They had come to America as children, or their parents had. They spoke English with an accent, and switched to the old language when they didn't want the children to understand. Their grandchildren often learned a few words, mostly the ones they weren't supposed to.""",
   "1950s immigrant grandmother portrait", "THE OLD LANGUAGE")
sc("""And grandparents connected children to history. A boy in nineteen sixty might have a grandfather who had seen Civil War veterans march in parades. A girl might have a grandmother who remembered when women couldn't vote. Women won the right to vote in nineteen twenty, and many grandmothers of nineteen sixty had cast their very first ballot that year.""",
   "1920 women voting suffrage", "SHE REMEMBERED")

ch("Holidays")
sc("""Holidays belonged to the grandparents. Thanksgiving and Christmas dinners at grandma's house were huge, with every aunt, uncle and cousin crammed into the dining room, and the kids at a card table in the kitchen. Grandma cooked for days beforehand. The turkey, the stuffing, the pies, and her famous rolls.""",
   "1950s family Thanksgiving dinner grandparents", "THE KIDS' TABLE")
sc("""After dinner, the men fell asleep in front of the football game. The women washed dishes and talked in the kitchen. The cousins ran wild outside, or explored the attic. And grandpa told the same stories he told every year, and everyone listened anyway.""",
   "1950s family living room after dinner", "THE SAME STORIES")
sc("""At Christmas, grandparents often gave practical gifts. Hand-knitted mittens and scarves. Socks. A savings bond, which the grandchildren didn't appreciate until years later. A crisp dollar bill in a card. And maybe one special toy, bought after weeks of saving. Those simple gifts are often the ones grandchildren remember the most.""",
   "1950s Christmas grandmother gift child", "A SAVINGS BOND")

ch("Growing old in 1960")
sc("""Growing old in nineteen sixty was harder than today in many ways. Life expectancy at birth was about seventy years. Heart disease, strokes and cancer took many people in their sixties. Hearing aids were big and clumsy. Eyeglasses were thick. And there were fewer treatments for the problems of old age.""",
   "1950s elderly man eyeglasses reading", "HARDER IN MANY WAYS")
sc("""When grandparents grew too old or sick to live alone, they usually moved in with their children. Nursing homes existed, but they were much less common than today. Families took care of their own. Daughters, especially, cared for aging parents, often for years. It was simply expected.""",
   "1950s daughter caring elderly mother", "FAMILIES TOOK CARE OF THEIR OWN")
sc("""And when a grandparent died, the funeral was often held in the family home, or at the local funeral parlor, with a wake the night before. The whole town came. Neighbors brought food. Children saw death up close, as a natural part of life. And the old house was sold, and the family scattered a little more.""",
   "1950s small town church funeral", "THE WHOLE TOWN CAME")

ch("What they gave")
sc("""So what did the grandparents of nineteen sixty give us? They gave us roots. A connection to a world that was already disappearing. They gave us their stories, their recipes, their quilts and their values. Hard work. Thrift. Faith. Family. Not complaining.""",
   "1950s grandparents family portrait", "THEY GAVE US ROOTS")
sc("""And many of us didn't realize how much they meant until they were gone. We wish we had asked more questions. Written down the recipes. Recorded their voices. Learned the old language. If your grandparents are still in your memory, maybe it's time to write down what you remember, for your own grandchildren.""",
   "old family photographs album", "WRITE IT DOWN")
sc("""So tell us in the comments. What do you remember most about your grandparents? What did your grandmother cook, and what did your grandfather teach you? We read every comment, and we'd love to hear about them. Next time, we're looking at what a dollar really bought in nineteen fifty-eight. Thank you for spending this time with me.""",
   "1950s grandmother hugging child porch", "TELL US ABOUT THEM")

